CODEGENGAPS - expressions the compiler cannot build, with repro source

Every case below was verified against the installed compiler on
2026-08-15, compiled as:

	/usr/local/bin/ccc -m micronix -O -c repro.c

All of them came out of porting the 2.11BSD userland (cat tee cmp ln
chmod chgrp du date dd mv pr od grep sed ...) - real code the PDP-11
compiler swallowed and this one does not.  Each entry gives the
smallest source that still fails, the exact diagnostic, and the
workaround the port used, so the ported sources can be unwound when
the rule is added.

Three kinds, and the distinction is the point of the layout: WRONG
CODE entries compile silently and compute the wrong thing - those
are the ones that cost afternoons; "no rule" entries fail loudly on
legitimate expressions; and the last section is refusals the
compiler is entitled to, recorded only so the diagnostic is
recognized on sight.

FIXED entries keep their repro and their diagnosis: the repro is the
regression test, and the diagnosis is why the next one like it will
be found faster.  The workarounds noted in each can be unwound in the
ported sources.

===========================================================================
c1: no rule to build code for
===========================================================================

The diagnostic in every c1 case is:

	1 expression(s) that no rule could build code for - the output
	is missing them
	Error: c1 failed on <file>

1. &= with a complemented bare variable  [FIXED]
------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit a6ac8a3.  NOT had forms for hl, bc and a and
none for de.  The complement is done in place now, result staying in
de where AND,INBC,INDE can take it.

Found porting chmod (the `=` case of symbolic modes).  Any
`x &= ~var` where the operand of ~ is a bare variable; parenthesized
expression forms like `x &= ~(a & b)` compile fine.

	f(a, m)
	int a, m;
	{
		a &= ~m;
		return a;
	}

Workaround in cmd/chmod: `nm ^= nm & m` computes the same value.

2. pointer subtraction with a register variable on the right  [FIXED]
---------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 0c89980.  Every register form of MINUS
carried the register on the left; here the address is.

Found porting pr (nexbuf()'s "how much room is left" computation).

	char buffer[512];
	f()
	{
		register char *p;

		p = buffer;
		return &buffer[512] - p;
	}

Workaround in cmd/pr: take the difference from the global before
copying it into the register:

	n = &buffer[BUFS] - bufp;
	rbufp = bufp;

3. *p compared with an address, p a register char ** from &arr[i]  [FIXED]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 0c89980.  Needed ASSIGN,DEREF,SYMREF,REGVAR -
a symbol's address stored through a register pointer.  The INHL and
P_NUM forms were beside it.

Found porting pr (tpgetc()'s column-pointer wraparound).  This one is
an interaction: the failure needs BOTH the pointer-to-pointer loaded
from an address-of-subscript AND the comparison, and small changes to
either side make it compile.

Fails:

	char buffer[9000];
	char *colp[72];
	f(i)
	int i;
	{
		register char **p;

		p = &colp[i];
		if (*p >= buffer)
			*p = buffer;
		return 0;
	}

Also fails with the comparison spelled `*p == &buffer[9000]`.

Compiles, for calibration - each is the same function with one thing
changed:

	p = colp + i;   ... if (*p >= buffer)     same value, + not &[]
	p = &colp[i];   ... if (*p == buffer)     equality vs bare array
	p = colp;       ... if (*p >= buffer)     no index in the load

Workaround in cmd/pr: go through a plain (non-register) temporary:

	q = *p + 1;
	if (q >= &buffer[BUFS])
		q = buffer;
	*p = q;

4. pointer plus one constant minus another, register pointer LHS  [FIXED]
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 911be51.  Not a rule: pass1 folds (x + c1) -
c2 into one offset now, so the shape never reaches the table.

Found porting grep (the -y buffer-overrun guard).  The unfolded
constant arithmetic on the right needs a register pointer on the
left to fail; with a plain parameter it compiles.

	char ybuf[256];
	f(a)
	char *a;
	{
		register char *s;

		s = a;
		if (s >= ybuf+256-5)
			return 1;
		return 0;
	}

Compiles, for calibration: `s >= ybuf+251`, `s >= &ybuf[251]`,
`s >= ybuf+256`, and the exact failing expression with `s` a plain
(non-register) parameter.

Workaround in cmd/grep: `&ybuf[ESIZE-5]` - the subscript form folds
the integers before the address is formed.

5. &= of the complement of a negate  [FIXED]
--------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit a6ac8a3.  Needed AND,INBC,INHL: and is
commutative but the matcher is not, and only the hl,bc spelling was
there.

Found porting od (the -w power-of-two clamp).  A cousin of entry 1.

	f()
	{
		register int w;

		w = 6;
		while (w &= (~(-w)))
			;
		return w;
