6. WRONG CODE: long minus an inline address-of-subscript difference  [HALF FIXED]
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HALF FIXED 2026-08-15, commit a71fa8e - the pass1 half.  The scaling
shortcut for byte-sized elements returned before typing the
difference, so it kept its POINTER type; the enclosing subtraction
then took the pointer-arithmetic path and narrowed the long operand
to sixteen bits.  The tree is right now and char and int pointers
behave alike.

STILL WRONG: the answer is 65337 rather than 77, and it was 65337 for
int pointers before any of this - the char case only reached the same
bug.  Given

	(SUB:long (DEREF:long nd)
		  (SEXT:long (SUB:short (ADD buf 33) (REGVAR BC))))

c1 emits the long subtraction and never computes the right operand -
push hl with nothing loaded into hl - and reports no error.  Rules for
SEXT to long from INHL, INBC and INA all exist, so something matches
at the SUB:long and consumes the child without emitting it.  That is
where the remaining wrong number lives.

Found porting od (odcvt's digit-count arithmetic).  THIS ONE IS NOT
AN ERROR - IT COMPILES AND RUNS AND PRODUCES THE WRONG NUMBER, the
only silent-wrong-code case the 2.11 sweep found.  A pointer
difference whose left side is an inline &arr[CONST], subtracted into
a long, comes out zero.

	char buf[33];
	main()
	{
		char *b;
		long t, nd;

		b = &buf[10];
		nd = 100;
		t = nd - (&buf[33] - b);
		printf("%ld\n", t);
		return 0;
	}

Prints 0.  The answer is 77.  The compound form `t = nd;
t -= &buf[33] - b;` also prints 0.  Verified by running both under
usersim on 2026-08-15.

Correct, for calibration - each prints 77:

	e = &buf[33];  t = nd - (e - b);      named pointer, no inline &[]
	d = &buf[33] - b;  t = nd;  t -= d;   difference into an int first

In od's original function (many long locals, six register variables)
the related shapes flipped between "no rule" errors and this silent
zero as register allocation moved; cmd/od's odcvt is rewritten one
plain statement a time with an emitted-digit counter instead of the
closing pointer subtraction.  Until this is fixed, any pointer
difference written against an inline &arr[CONST] and mixed into long
arithmetic is suspect.

7. WRONG CODE: pointer pre-increment inside a comparison scales by 1  [FIXED]
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit d5315d2.  cmpunsigned() retypes a pointer
operand to unsigned short so ordered comparisons go unsigned, and the
step was being recovered afterwards, at emission, from the type->sub
it had just discarded.  mkIncDec records the step when the node is
built, where the type is still the operand's own.

Found porting sed (`if (++rep >= ptrend)` over struct pointers).
The increment happens, but by one BYTE, not one element - no
diagnostic, just a pointer walking through the middles of its
elements.  `p++;` as a statement and the deref forms (`*++p`,
`*p++ = v`) scale correctly.

	int arr[10];
	main()
	{
		register int *p, *lim;
		int n;

		p = arr;
		lim = &arr[3];
		n = 0;
		while (++p < lim)
			n++;
		printf("%d\n", n);
		return 0;
	}

Prints 5.  The answer is 2: six bytes of distance walked a byte at
a time instead of an int at a time.  Verified under usersim on
2026-08-15.  sed hit it on struct pointers, where the stride error
is even larger.

Workaround in cmd/sed: split it - `rep++; if (rep >= ptrend)`.

8. WRONG CODE: table-copy loop with three register char pointers  [FIXED]
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 6ac17c1.  Not the three pointers: the byte
store through a pointer in a frame slot (ASSIGN,DEREF,INA,INDEX) gave
destval 0, so the assignment's value came from e->tgt - the pointer
stored through - instead of the byte stored.  The loop tested an
address, which is never zero.  The third register variable only
mattered because it decided whether the allocator spilled the pointer
and so which of two rules fired.  destval is R_A now.

Found porting sed (the y-command's translate loop).  The loop is
correct with two register char pointers in the frame and an
infinite loop with three - the third need not even appear in the
loop.

	char tab[256];
	char line[20];
	main()
	{
		register int i;
		register char *p1, *p2, *p3;

		for (i = 1; i < 256; i++)
			tab[i] = i;
		strcpy(line, "abc");
		p1 = line;
		p2 = tab;
		p3 = tab;
		while (*p1 = p2[*p1])
			p1++;
		printf("%s\n", line);
		return 0;
	}

Hangs.  Delete `p3` (and its assignment) and it prints abc and
exits.  Verified under usersim on 2026-08-15, 15-second timeout
against the three-register build.

Workaround in cmd/sed: the loop is hoisted into a translate(lp, tp)
helper whose frame declares only two register pointers.

9. assignment as the value of a pointer comparison, register struct  [FIXED]
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART FIXED 2026-08-15, commit fd41586.  Needed ASSIGN,DEREF,INDE,REGVAR:
a word stored from de through a register pointer.  The store's VALUE is
in de because the other operand of the comparison has taken hl, and
the table had the hl, bc and constant forms and not that one.

FIXED 2026-08-15.  The global-pointer half was not a missing rule.
ASSIGN,DEREF,REGVAR,SYMREF was in the table the whole time and had
been storing correctly for as long as it had been there - with
destval 0.  A rule that names no result register gives the rewrite
nothing to relabel the ASSIGN to, so the store reduced and the
COMPARISON over it did not, and the whole expression came out as no
rule.  The refusal read as a missing rule; the rule that was wrong
was present, and silent.

Its siblings say what they hold - the INHL store says R_HL, the INDE
store says R_DE - and this one said nothing, which is the shape of
every gap in this file: one spelling of a family left unfilled.
Four were unfilled the same way, and now say where their value is:

	ASSIGN,DEREF,REGVAR,SYMREF	store ix through a global
	ASSIGN,DEREF,REGVAR,INBC	store ix through a bc pointer
	ASSIGN,DEREF,INBC,REGVAR	store bc through (ix+0)
	ASSIGN,DEREF,INA,REGVAR		store a through (ix+0), byte

The first two borrow hl to reach the address and now save it: an
assignment used as a value sits under an operator that has already
evaluated its other operand into hl, and the compare was reading
what the store had left there.

All six of the family now name their value.  Two of them - the forms
whose address ARRIVES in hl (INHL, INDEX) - could not until the
ordering was fixed, because whatever computed that address had run
through the same hl the operator above was using.  That is entry 15,
and it is what sed was really hitting; both were needed.

	char *reend;
	struct r {
		char *ad2;
	};
	struct r *rep;
	f()
	{
		register char *p;

		p = reend;
		if ((rep->ad2 = p) > reend)
			return 1;
		return 0;
	}

Verified fixed 2026-08-15 on the simulator - store and comparison
both, through a global pointer, a register pointer and (ix+0), high
and low against reend.  cmd/sed's two split sites (marked
"CODEGENGAPS 9" in sed0.c) can come back together.

The register struct pointer is not the discriminator - a file-scope
one fails identically.  Which SIDE the store lands on is:

	(rep->ad2 = p) < reend		compiled
	reend > (rep->ad2 = p)		compiled - GT swaps, store left
	reend < (rep->ad2 = p)		failed - store right, value in de

and the entry's own spelling is the third, GT normalizing to LT by
exchanging its operands.

Two wrong diagnoses on the way, recorded because the METHOD is the
lesson.  cmpunsigned was blamed for installing an unsigned width no
rule could match: pnode compares widths case-insensitively, so it
cannot matter.  Then the store was said to be steered to de and
unable to reduce: the store is targeted to hl, and it is its value
that arrives in de.  Both came of reasoning about the source and the
rule table.  Printing the tree where reduction stops - dumpexpr at
the unreduced marker in lower.c - answered it in one look, and is
what to do first next time.

===========================================================================
c0: front end
===========================================================================

Not codegen, but real bugs: maximal munch makes both of these legal
source - `argc-->1` is `argc--  >  1` and nothing else - however
much the spelling deserves what it gets.  The ports keep the spaces
regardless.

Both are ONE bug, in cpp's gettoken() (src/cpp/lex.c, the operator
scan at the bottom): after the doubled-character branch builds `--`
or `++`, the following suffix checks still run, and they test the
ORIGINAL first character rather than the token built so far.  So
the arrow check (`c == '-' && curchar == '>'`) rewrites a finished
`--` into `->` when a `>` follows, and the `=`-suffix check
(lookupc(eqChars, c)) rewrites a finished `++` into `+=` when a `=`
follows - which is exactly what the two diagnostics below show the
parser receiving.  `>>=`/`<<=` are already special-cased inside the
doubled branch; the fix is for a doubled match to end the operator
scan instead of falling through.

10. --> is mis-tokenized  [FIXED]
---------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 9c78df2, with entry 11 - one bug.  A doubled
match now ends the operator scan.

Found porting tee, which counts arguments with `while(argc-->1)`.
The lexer loses the parse; the statement never recovers.

	f(argc)
	int argc;
	{
		int n;

		n = 0;
		while (argc-->1)
			n++;
		return n;
	}

	arrow.c:8: expr paren
	arrow.c:8: switch {
	arrow.c:10: need }
	bad op (member): 21

Workaround: `argc-- > 1`.  The space is load-bearing and the ported
source says so at the site.

11. ++== is mis-tokenized the same way  [FIXED]
-----------------------------------------------
FIXED 2026-08-15, commit 9c78df2, with entry 10.

Found porting od: `if (*p++=='.')`.  Same disease as entry 10, the
