	plusplus.c:7: bad op
	plusplus.c:7: need lvalue

Workaround: the space before ==.

===========================================================================
limitations, not bugs - the source is what should change
===========================================================================

The compiler refuses these and is entitled to.  They are recorded so
the next port recognizes the diagnostic and fixes the source without
a diagnosis detour; nobody should teach c0 to like them.

12. storage class after the type
--------------------------------
Found porting tee: `int register r, w, p;`.  K&R technically lets
the storage class and type come in either order; c0 only accepts
the class first, and the class-first spelling is the only one that
should exist anyway.

	f()
	{
		int register r;

		r = 1;
		return r;
	}

	regorder.c:3: bad decl token
	...
	regorder.c:5: undefined symbol

Fix the source: `register int r`.

13. static definition after an implicit-int call
------------------------------------------------
Found porting date.  Calling an undeclared function declares it
extern int; a later definition that says static disagrees with that
linkage, and c0 says so.  The PDP-11 compiler let it slide and 2.11
code leans on the slack, but the complaint is on the source.

	main()
	{
		return g();
	}
	static
	g()
	{
		return 1;
	}

	staticg.c:8: redecl disagrees

Fix the source: declare `static g();` before the first call, or
drop the static.

===========================================================================
not the compiler, but found the same way
===========================================================================

libc's doprnt.c defines a GLOBAL icvt()  [NOT REPRODUCIBLE]
-----------------------------------------------------------
Checked 2026-08-15: icvt is already static in doprnt.c and no icvt
symbol appears in libc.a.  Either it was fixed after this was written
or the collision od hit came from somewhere else.  Left here with its
reasoning because the link-order argument below is still sound and
worth knowing if a duplicate ever does appear.

src/libc/doprnt.c line 23: icvt() is the number formatter doprnt
uses internally, and it is a global.  Any program that defines its
own icvt() collides with it - od did, taken verbatim from 2.11, and
its link failed on the duplicate (a minimal two-function repro links
without complaint, so the failure depends on link order and what
else the archive pulls in - which makes it worse, not better: when
the link DOES succeed, one of the two functions has silently won).
cmd/od renamed its copy odcvt.  The fix is `static` on doprnt's
icvt, which frees the name and costs nothing.


15. an assignment used as a value, through an address computed in hl  [FIXED]
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Found 2026-08-15 while closing entry 9, and it turned out to be what
sed was really hitting.  The rules there were a real gap and fixing
them was necessary, but sed's own shape needed this as well:

	if((rep->ad2 = p) > reend)	rep global, ad2 at offset 2,
					p spilled to the frame

	LT(DEREF $_reend, ASSIGN(ADD(DEREF $_rep, 2), DEREF(LOCALVAR IY-10)))

The comparison loaded reend into hl, then worked the address out on
top of it - ld hl,(_rep) / inc hl / inc hl - and both operands ended
up claiming hl, which the table has no rule for and rightly not.

An ordering problem, not a spelling one.  label() had:

	case ASSIGN:
		e->regs = r ? r : 1;	/* lvalue doesn't consume reg */

The lvalue is free when its address is named in the instruction - a
global, a register pointer, (ix+d) - and not free when it is
arithmetic.  DEREF carries this guard already, three cases above,
raising itself to 2 so that it goes first "before an operand is
sitting in HL to be clobbered".  ASSIGN now takes the larger of its
two sides, which is all that was wanted:

	case ASSIGN:
		e->regs = r ? r : 1;
		if (l > e->regs)
			e->regs = l;

The assignment then outranks the operand beside it, gets evaluated
first, and the compare loads reend afterwards into the register the
store did not want.

An earlier attempt keyed on the address's SHAPE instead - a helper
asking whether it was a subscript - and never fired once.  label() is
called through reduction rather than once ahead of it, and by the
time this ASSIGN is labeled its address has already reduced to INHL,
which honestly costs nothing, the work having been done.  The count
has to come from the child's own label, which is what taking l does.

With the ordering right, all six rules of the store family can name
their value, including the two whose address arrives in hl.  Checked
on the simulator - store and comparison both - through a global
pointer, a register pointer, (ix+0) and a subscript, high and low
against reend; and cmd/sed builds with both split sites unwound and
runs its ranges and substitutions correctly.
